ry  dark place that i thought i would never  get out of but um  eventually um about three months ago  i was able to get out of that and live a  happy and sober life  are you finding life as a transgender is  easier  on the west coast than it is absolutely  becky's  yeah i i lived in california about four  years ago  um which is part of why um i wanted to  leave new york and go to a treatment  facility that was in california  because i it was the atmosphere was just  so much more  um friendly and pleasant more accepting  accepting yes  are drugs a part of the picture for you  not anymore um  since then i i've you know i've been  sober um  from uh alcohol and heroin for about  three months now  where are you living now um i live in a  sober house  for you and how do you support yourself  um right now um my parents uh had a  trust fund set up for me  so i'm using money from that until i can  get a job which is very hard with  everything going on right now  so yeah that was a  tough time for everybody yeah  [Music]  have you had rough experiences being  transgender people would  um i have yes um  you know just like the discrimination of  like just  things people walking down the street  say to me or like  you know not getting jobs or i remember  even like in high school i faced a lot  of discrimination even from teachers and  students  and you know there's rehabs  treatment rehab centers that i've been  to where i had to face discrimination  even from a place that was supposed to  be helping me  but discriminated against me and said  and um  you know it was very it's been very  difficult you know  but um i've learned to um love myself  so you know whenever i do happen to face  discrimination i just let it go  because i know that um  i'm just a better person and that it  doesn't matter what other people say  about you  good for you that's got to be a real  challenge yeah  it's nice to hear thank you tell me  about the tattoo on your forehead  um i was homeless in denver 